---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadFilms } from '../lib/films.mjs';

// The 2015 Oscar films Laurel and Joe tested (87th Academy Awards). Scores are
// from that study; two of the films are also on today's shelf.
const STUDY = [
	['Boyhood', 3],
	['Gone Girl', 3],
	['The Imitation Game', 3],
	['Into the Woods', 3],
	['Wild', 3],
	['American Sniper', 2],
	['Birdman', 1],
	['Foxcatcher', 1],
	['The Grand Budapest Hotel', 1],
	['Still Alice', 1],
	['The Theory of Everything', 1],
	['The Judge', 1],
	['Whiplash', 1],
] as const;

const onShelf = new Map(loadFilms().map((f) => [f.title, f.slug]));
const rows = STUDY.map(([title, score]) => ({ title, score, slug: onShelf.get(title) ?? null }));
const passing = rows.filter((r) => r.score === 3).length;
---

<Base
	title="Case study: the 2015 Oscar films"
	description="How the films nominated at the 2015 Oscars score on the Bechdel test: 5 of the 13 we could test pass, and only 2 of the 7 Best Picture nominees."
	nav="case-study"
>
	<div class="page">
		<h1 class="page-title">Case study: the 2015 Oscar films</h1>
		<p class="page-lead">{passing} of the {rows.length} films we could test pass. Only 2 of the 7 Best Picture nominees do.</p>

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="scores-title">
			<h2 id="scores-title" class="section-title">The scores</h2>
			<ul class="legend">
				<li><span class="swatch swatch-w"></span>A rule the film meets</li>
				<li><span class="swatch empty"></span>A rule it misses</li>
			</ul>
			<table class="scores">
				<caption class="sr-only">Bechdel scores of 13 films nominated at the 2015 Oscars, out of 3</caption>
				<thead class="sr-only">
					<tr><th scope="col">Film</th><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Verdict</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						rows.map((r) => (
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">{r.slug ? <a href={`/films/${r.slug}/`}>{r.title}</a> : r.title}</th>
								<td>
									<span class="marks" aria-hidden="true">
										{[0, 1, 2].map((i) => (
											<span class:list={['mark', { on: i < r.score }]} />
										))}
									</span>
									<span class="sr-only">{r.score} of 3</span>
								</td>
								<td class:list={['verdict', r.score === 3 ? 'pass' : 'fail']}>{r.score === 3 ? 'Passes' : 'Fails'}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</section>

		<div class="prose">
			<h2>What we found</h2>
			<p>
				The Bechdel test, popularized by cartoonist Alison Bechdel, asks whether a film has a scene with two women talking to each
				other about something other than a man. It's often used to show the sexism in the film industry, because so many films fail
				such a low bar. Feminism is far more accepted than it used to be, but men still hold most speaking roles on screen, and most
				of the directing and writing jobs behind it.
			</p>
			<p>
				To see where the industry stands, we tested 13 films nominated at the 87th Academy Awards (February 22, 2015) for Best
				Picture, Best Actor, Best Actress, Best Supporting Actor and Best Supporting Actress.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>5 of the 13 films pass, about 38%.</li>
				<li>Only 2 of the 7 Best Picture nominees we tested pass, about 29%.</li>
				<li>Every Best Picture nominee has a man in the lead.</li>
				<li>Half the films with a Best Actress nominee pass, against 1 in 5 films with a Best Actor nominee.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Best Picture is the best overview of the year's acclaimed films, because it isn't tied to the gender of the lead the way
				Best Actor and Best Actress are. When the Academy is free to pick the best films of the year, its picks are about men.
			</p>
			<p>
				Only one film with a Best Actress nominee was also up for Best Picture: <cite>The Theory of Everything</cite>. Four films
				with a Best Actor nominee were: <cite>The Theory of Everything</cite>, <cite>American Sniper</cite>,
				<cite>The Imitation Game</cite> and <cite>Birdman</cite>. When every Best Picture nominee is led by a man, the Academy has
				to look outside that list to find lead actresses.
			</p>
			<p>
				The same imbalance holds for the supporting roles. 1 in 5 films with a Best Supporting Actor nominee pass, against 3 in 5
				with a Best Supporting Actress nominee. When a story is about men, women rarely share a scene.
			</p>
			<p>
				When most of a year's most acclaimed films fail the test, the problem runs through the whole industry. Lead roles go to men
				far more often, and few women write or direct Oscar-nominated films. To get better roles for women, women need to be making
				the films.
			</p>

			<h2>How we tested</h2>
			<p>
				Two nominees are missing. The script for <cite>Selma</cite>, a Best Picture nominee, wasn't publicly available. <cite>Two
				Days, One Night</cite>, with Best Actress nominee Marion Cotillard, is in French, and its script was only available in
				French, which our parser can't read.
			</p>
			<p><a href="/">Browse every film we've tested</a></p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.scores {
		border-collapse: collapse;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 760px;
	}
	.scores th,
	.scores td {
		padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3) var(--space-2) 0;
		text-align: left;
	}
	.scores tbody th {
		font-family: var(--poster);
		font-weight: 400;
		font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}
	.scores tbody th a {
		color: var(--chalk);
	}
	.marks {
		display: inline-flex;
		gap: var(--mark-gap);
	}
	.mark {
		width: 22px;
		height: 22px;
		border: 2px solid var(--muted);
	}
	.mark.on {
		background: var(--pink);
		border-color: var(--pink);
	}
	.swatch.empty {
		border: 2px solid var(--muted);
	}
	.verdict {
		font-family: var(--poster);
		font-size: 1.25rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}
	.verdict.pass {
		color: var(--pink);
	}
	.verdict.fail {
		color: var(--tangerine);
	}
</style>
